== the Windows failure this time is in the repository, not in the runner

  error[E0425]: cannot find function `process_not_running` in the crate root
     --> crates\codegen\xai-tty-utils\src\kill_on_drop_tests.rs:21:19
  note: found an item that was configured out
     --> crates\codegen\xai-tty-utils\src\lib.rs:549:8
  ...
  ##[error]Process completed with exit code 101.

This time the ripgrep step passed and `cargo test` ran; it failed to build one test
target. `kill_on_drop_tests.rs` is compiled on every platform but calls
`xai_tty_utils::process_not_running`, which was `#[cfg(unix)]`-only, so the crate's test
binary has never existed on Windows. The same file's fixtures also spawned `sleep 300` and
`true`, i.e. Unix userland, so gating the module would have traded a red leg for a leg that
checks nothing.

The fix adds the Windows side of the probe (`OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION)`
plus `GetExitCodeProcess`; an unopenable PID counts as gone, an unreadable one reports
"running" so a caller notices) and gives the fixtures a process that exists on both
platforms (`sleep 300` / `ping -n 300 127.0.0.1`, and `true` / `cmd /C exit 0`), both
spawned directly so the guarded PID is the process that gets killed. Four `unused_imports`
warnings in the same crate's test code -- imports whose only users are Linux-only tests --
were gated too, because that leg had reported `xai-tty-utils (lib test) generated 4
warnings`.

Checked locally before pushing: `cargo check -p xai-tty-utils --all-targets` for
x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, aarch64-pc-windows-msvc and x86_64-apple-darwin each finish with no
warnings and no errors, `cargo test -p xai-tty-utils` is green on Linux (62 + 1 + 2), and
`cargo clippy -p xai-tty-utils --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean.

What this file is still NOT evidence about: a Windows run of those three tests. The
cross-compile says the target builds; only the next `platform tests (windows-latest)` leg
says whether `KillOnDrop` behaves there.

## Run 37014349712 (commit 834d19e0) and why `installer integrity labs` could not go green

Observed at 15:05 UTC on the run for `834d19e0`:

| job | outcome |
|---|---|
| workflows present | success |
| docs localization | success |
| secret scan | success |
| npm package scripts | success |
| GUI browser E2E | success |
| GUI engine, Web host and frontend | success |
| installer integrity labs | failure, 15 s |
| rust check / clippy / test | in progress at the time of writing |
| platform tests (macos/windows) | not started; they `need:` the rust job |

The failing job died in its prerequisite step, so both lab steps were skipped:

```
   step: Install the crypto binding the fixture signs with
   pwsh --version                     -> PowerShell 7.6.6
   unshare -rn true
   unshare: write failed /proc/self/uid_map: Operation not permitted
   ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.
```

That answers the question this file had been carrying about whether `unshare -rn` works
on GitHub-hosted ubuntu runners: it does not. The images have unprivileged user-namespace
creation off, and the probe was written into the step that installs the crypto binding,
so refusing it took down the sibling lab in the same job -- `install-integrity-in-docker.sh`,
which isolates with `docker run --network none` and needs no user namespace at all. One
runner limitation was being reported as two lab failures.

The fix keeps the property the job was designed around -- neither lab may go green by
absence, so an unavailable precondition exits 2 naming the reason -- and moves the
decision into the lab that needs it:

- The workflow step prints which route the machine allows and asserts nothing.
- `install-integrity-powershell.sh` resolves a route itself: `unshare -rn` where an
  unprivileged user namespace is allowed, otherwise `sudo -n unshare -n`.
- The root route is `unshare -n` and not `unshare -rn`, which was established by running
  it rather than reasoned about. With `-r` the lab lands in a child user namespace, where
  a process has no privileges over resources outside it; on this machine the repository
  sits under a `drwxr-x---` home directory and the inner half failed with
  `Permission denied` reading its own script. Root with a network namespace only keeps
  normal access to the tree, and can still bring `lo` up because the new namespace
  belongs to root's own user namespace.

Both routes were then run for real, on a machine that allows both, via
`CHAOS_PS1_LAB_NS_ROUTE` -- which exists so the CI-only branch is testable somewhere
instead of being shipped untested:

```
CHAOS_PS1_LAB_NS_ROUTE=unshare  -> all 30 PowerShell release-integrity checks passed
CHAOS_PS1_LAB_NS_ROUTE=sudo     -> all 30 PowerShell release-integrity checks passed
```

In each transcript the two premises that make the run meaningful are the same:

```
   ok  loopback is up, which is all the fake release endpoint needs
   ok  github.com cannot be resolved from this namespace (curl exit 6)
```

so the root route is not a weaker isolation with a green tick on top: the installer still
provably cannot reach the real feed. The lab directory was removed in both runs
(`ls /tmp/chaos-install-integrity-ps1.*` empty afterwards), which the root route has to
earn by cleaning up as the same user that wrote it -- the cleanup trap lives in the
inner half, which is the half that runs as root.

A third runner defect is recorded alongside this one: every Docker lab in this repository
kept its containers alive with `--entrypoint sleep <image> 3600`, so the container's PID 1
exited after one hour and SIGKILLed whichever `docker exec` was in flight.
`install-sh-in-docker.sh` hit it on a 150 MB release download and reported
`install.sh exited 137 using only its built-in key`, plus two "no OK line" failures, all of
which attributed a harness timeout to the installer. The ceiling is now `CHAOS_LAB_KEEPALIVE`
(six hours, printed at start), kept finite on purpose so an interrupted run cannot leave a
container behind forever.

What this file is still NOT evidence about: a green `installer integrity labs` job on a
runner. The routes are proven on Linux here; the next push's run is what shows the job
passing where it previously could not.

