The WSL-launcher diagnosis in `docs/verification/installer-asset-probe-2026-10-03.log`
did its job -- `find_bash()` is in this head (`git show 22683f8c:scripts/ci/test-installer-asset-names.py`
contains it) and the step now gets a usable interpreter:

    ok  executing install.sh's detect_platform with C:\Program Files\Git\bin\bash.exe
    ok  every asset arm names a published asset when run: Linux/x86_64->chaos-linux-x64,
        Linux/aarch64->chaos-linux-arm64, Darwin/x86_64->chaos-darwin-x64, Darwin/arm64->chaos-darwin-arm64
    ok  and is refused as designed on MINGW64_NT-10.0, FreeBSD, Linux
    note  this host's uname is a Windows one, so detect_platform refuses, as designed
    8 check(s), 0 failure(s)

What fails is the fixture that runs next, not the check:

    FAIL: test_the_rest_of_the_check_still_runs_when_bash_is_found (__main__.DegradedLookupTests.test_the_rest_of_the_check_still_runs_when_bash_is_found)
    Traceback (most recent call last):
      File "D:\a\chaos-code\chaos-code\scripts\ci\test-installer-bash-resolution.py", line 182, in test_the_rest_of_the_check_still_runs_when_bash_is_found
        self.assertIn("this host's own uname", out.getvalue())
    AssertionError: "this host's own uname" not found in "   ok  release.yml publishes 6 assets: ...
        \n   note  this host's uname is a Windows one, so detect_platform refuses, as designed\n"
    Ran 13 tests in 1.610s
    FAILED (failures=1, skipped=10)

This is a defect in the test this repository added on 2026-10-03 to stop exactly this
kind of Windows breakage, and the Windows leg is what found it. The install.sh leg of
the asset-name check has two terminal sentences and the host decides which one runs:

    res.note("this host's uname is a Windows one, so detect_platform refuses, as designed")
    res.ok(f"this host's own uname picks {host.stdout.strip()!r}, which is published")

The fixture asserted the second one unconditionally, so it was a Linux-only test wearing
a cross-platform name. Every one of its 12 siblings passed on Windows; this one had
never been run anywhere but Linux. Writing "the Windows leg is now unblocked" without
running it is the same mistake one fewer step earlier.

Fix. The host leg's outcome is not the test's choice, so the test must stop pinning one
wording and instead require that exactly one of the two was reported:

    def assertHostOutcome(self, out: str) -> str:
        chosen = [marker for marker in (POSIX_HOST_OUTCOME, WINDOWS_HOST_OUTCOME) if marker in out]
        self.assertEqual(len(chosen), 1, ...)
        return chosen[0]

and the two sentences are pinned separately, by driving the branch on any host. The
probe arms call `run_detect_platform` while the host leg calls `run_shipped`, so
monkeypatching only the latter selects the branch without disturbing anything else:

    for stderr, want in (
        ("", POSIX_HOST_OUTCOME),
        ("install.sh: this host needs install.ps1 -- use PowerShell to install\n", WINDOWS_HOST_OUTCOME),
    ):
        ...
        asset_names.run_shipped = lambda *_a, **_k: subprocess.CompletedProcess(
            args=["bash"], returncode=0 if not stderr else 1, stdout="chaos-linux-x64", stderr=stderr)

Proof against the captured CI output, rather than against a guess about it: the verbatim
Windows transcript above was fed to the new assertion.

    old assertion "this host's own uname" present? False
    new assertion accepts it and picks: "this host's uname is a Windows one"
    picks (POSIX run): "this host's own uname picks"
    rejected as expected: 0 != 1 : the host uname leg must report exactly one outcome, got [] from:
    rejected as expected: 2 != 1 : the host uname leg must report exactly one outcome, got [...] from:

Five mutations, each written back and `cmp`-verified byte-identical; 0 survived:

| # | mutation | fixture that goes red |
|---|---|---|
| B1 | pinned Windows sentence wrong (`...is a BSD one`) | `test_each_host_outcome_is_reachable_and_pinned` |
| B2 | fake never refuses, so the Windows branch is unreachable | same |
| B3 | the checker rewords its Windows note | same |
| B4 | the Windows refusal stops being a note (`if False and …`) | same |
| B5 | the selection stops selecting (markers not filtered by presence) | `test_each_host_outcome_is_reachable_and_pinned`, `test_the_rest_of_the_check_still_runs_when_bash_is_found` |

B1/B2/B3/B4 are the ones that matter for the original bug: they show the Windows branch
is genuinely driven here, and that a wording change on either side of the seam is caught
on Linux instead of on a Windows runner five pushes later. `Ran 14 tests ... OK` locally
after the change (13 before, plus the pinned-branch test).

What this section is still not evidence about

`cargo test (target-OS crates)` has still never executed on Windows. Step 9 is `skipped`
in this run just as in the four before it, so the honest count is: macOS has produced
real results, Windows has produced none, and the fifth consecutive head has a Windows
failure that is about our tooling rather than the product. Fixing step 3 makes step 9
reachable; it does not make it pass, and this row cannot be closed by anything recorded
in this file.
